>Can anybody *top* this long, sad story, or do I have the record for
>the most *misfortune* (as opposed to silliest or most arbitrary death)
>ever to befall a character?

Well, one can only try.

This happened a while ago, so some of the details may have gotten kind
of fuzzed out.  But the gist of it remains the same:

Esmerelda the Valkyrie was cruising.  This was my first real successful
game of 3.1, so I wasn't completely aware of the role of the Candelabra
et. al. (but figured it out).  Unfortunately, the lighting store was
infested with mimics and I had come away with four candles.  As a
result, Esmerelda was forced to just wandering around, hoping for
something to give her wishes (I'd spent all the ones from the WoW in the
castle, not knowing I would need seven candles), a wand of polymorph (to
polypile tools) or just some candles (HAH!).  I accumulated seven
amulets of life saving, had over four hundred hit points and Vorpal
Blade.  Wasn't much anything could do to me...or so I thought.  This was
a character who cleared Vlad's tower out with a lamp without even
realizing she was doing it (it seemed like it was tougher to kill
things, but I never bothered checking WHY until afterwards).  Now, the
sad part.

I'm mugging around in the Dungeons of Doom on around level twenty, bored
out of my skull.  So bored that I didn't notice the "Weak" in my bottom
right hand corner.  I get the "malignant aura" message and check Mr.
Inventory to see what has come up cursed NOW (understand that after
100000 turns or so, pretty much everything in one's inventory is
cursed).  Good haul:  my bag of holding that held my food and potions
and my ring of levitation (which I was stupidly wearing, having not
taken it off after blowing through the castle level).  Wotta pain.  Open
the bag of holding to get my holy water out (which is in VERY limited
supply these days).  Gone along with my rations.  NOW I'm mad.  Now the
Wizard of Yendor shows up.  Now he pulls "double trouble" on me.  Now he
summons monsters three times.  Did I mention I fainted from lack of food?

Life ain't easy when you are burdened, levitating, are surrounded by
dragons and Wizards of Yendor, are fainting from hunger, have angered
your god from accidently booting an altar (got a little enthusiastic
with the ^A command) and have no food.  Still, I have those seven
amulets of life saving and have plenty of hit points, so no biggie.  I
wake up and my actions consist of getting a scroll of remove curse out
from another bag of holding and reading it.  Okay, one problem solved. 
I faint again, and die from loss of hit points.  No biggie.  I wake up. 
I put on another amulet, take off the ring and faint again.  Wake up,
get a lembas wafer from my bag (nothing else disappeared, thank
goodness), faint again.  Die.  Wake up, put on another amulet (keep
score here, folks), take a bite of lembas wafer, teleport to the up
stairs and shake the exit stick.  I still have the two Wizards on my
tail and am still burdened, but at least I'm not getting reamed by
dragons.  I teleport to the fountain on the level, dip my bag to try and
uncurse it as the wizards show up.  I generate a water nymph.  A pain,
but what are the odds she'll successfully charm me?  Pretty slim, but
she did in nonetheless.

So there I am, sans shield of reflection and gray dragon scale mail,
feeling a bit sheepish.  I behead one Wizard and try dipping the bag
again, since I feel my luck has to change.  It does.  The bag uncurses
and I'm no longer burdened.  I start to go to town on the other wizard. 
He's got other plans, such as the finger of death.

Okay, now I'm getting steamed.  I put on another amulet, and make
another try at the Wizard.  He generates a twin, and then start
summoning monsters to beat the band.  Some chump bugbear who thinks he's
a big shot starts running amok with a wand of death and clips me.  Death
#4.

I put on ANOTHER amulet and decide I need my armor back (duh).  I check
the spell list and, sure enough, monster detection is one of the many
spellbooks I'd found and left behind.  A quick (Z)ap, a quick teleport,
and I'm about to work this nymph over for my armor.  Hmm, what's this? 
A gremlin?  What's this, he swiped TELEPORT CONTROL?  Argh.  Now I'm
hosed.  Suffice it to say that the rest of the story involved a hasty
retreat into hell, confusion, blundering into a high priest of Moloch,
lots of flailing, fainting, random teleporting and ended with an attempt
at prayer that resulted in a terminal bolt of lightening.  Killed by
holy wrath.  How appropriate.

